<mujoco model="overboard_onewheel">
  <!--
    Overboard onewheel plant model — driverless configuration.

    Topology is the real vehicle: a `frame` (deck, rails, enclosures, battery,
    electronics, bumpers, footpads) carried on a free joint, with the hub
    `wheel` attached to it by a single pitch-axis hinge. That hinge is the
    motor axis, so `wheel_motor` below is the one and only actuator — exactly
    the seam the Rust control stack drives through `hal::BoardIo`.

    NOTE — no rider, no mast, no proxy mass. An earlier checkpoint model faked
    instability with a tall rider-mass-on-a-stick ("ball on a stick") so the
    rig would topple on its own. That was a lie about the physics: a bare
    driverless onewheel at rest is *stable*, because the battery and hub motor
    put the centre of mass at or below the axle. Instability here is driven by
    an external disturbance instead — see sim/scenarios/impulse_response.py.

    NO AERODYNAMIC DRAG IN THIS FILE, DELIBERATELY. `overboard_rider.xml`
    adds a rider-silhouette fluid-drag geom (CdA ~0.6-0.9 m^2 per that
    file's comment) because a standing rider is most of a onewheel's
    frontal area. This file has no rider, and a bare board's own frontal
    area is a bumper/enclosure cross-section of well under 0.05 m^2 -- at
    the same drag-coefficient assumption that is under a tenth of the
    ridden CdA, smaller than the rolling-resistance term already on
    `wheel_hinge` at any speed this file's harnesses (impulse response,
    the Rust/Python replay-equivalence gate) run at. Modelling it would add
    an invented per-geom fluid geometry to a file whose whole point is
    topological fidelity to the real vehicle, for an effect below the
    other terms' own uncertainty band. Add it here, sized to the bare
    board, the day a driverless harness actually needs cruise-speed drag
    fidelity — do not import the rider CdA onto this vehicle in the
    meantime, which is the wrong-by-an-order-of-magnitude error this note
    exists to prevent.

    GEOMETRY / MESH PROVENANCE
    Openwheel meshes (MIT — see meshes/openwheel/NOTICE.md) are authored in
    millimetres in a shared assembly frame centred on the wheel axle, so mesh
    scale is 0.001 and no rotation is needed: mesh X/Y/Z already line up with
    the body's fore-aft/lateral/up axes. Verified against the raw STL bounding
    boxes — the front/rear enclosure and bumper pairs mirror exactly across
    X=0, and the gap between the enclosures is 290.8 mm, i.e. a 145.4 mm tire
    radius, which is the tire size used below. FORWARD IS -X (the front
    enclosure spans x = -431.8..-145.4 mm).

    The one exception is front_footpad.stl, which per its raw vertex
    coordinates was exported sitting at the *rear* footpad location rather
    than mirrored to the front; it is placed with an extra 180 deg yaw to move
    it to the front, matching front_enclosure/front_bumper.

    NOSE-STRIKE ANGLE (the topple criterion)
    With the axle held at the 145.4 mm tire radius, the underside of a bumper
    reaches the ground after 18.57 deg of pitch. So this vehicle physically
    CANNOT exceed ~18.6 deg of pitch while upright — it noses into the ground
    first. That, not an arbitrary 45 deg tilt, is the real failure mode and the
    real margin the balance controller has to hold.

    The bumper meshes are therefore collision-enabled (MuJoCo convex-hulls
    them) and the angle is computed from those hulls at runtime by
    `nose_strike_angle_deg()` — it is not a tuned constant, and it tracks any
    change to the meshes or the tire radius.

    Do NOT re-derive this from the STL bounding box. Doing so gives 14.9 deg,
    by assuming the extreme -X and extreme -Z coordinates meet at one corner.
    They do not: the bumper sweeps upward toward its tip, so the vertex that
    actually lands first is the underside heel at x = -381 mm, some 90 mm
    inboard of the 469 mm tip.
  -->
  <compiler angle="degree" meshdir="meshes/openwheel"/>

  <!-- Fixed timestep + RK4, no stochastic elements anywhere in the model:
       the scenario runner depends on bit-identical repeat runs. -->
  <option gravity="0 0 -9.81" timestep="0.002" integrator="RK4"/>

  <visual>
    <!-- 720p offscreen buffer: the scenario renderer films at 1280x720 and
         MuJoCo will not allocate beyond what is declared here. -->
    <global offwidth="1280" offheight="720"/>
    <!-- Low ambient so the directional key light actually models the form;
         at 0.45 the whole scene flattened out and the ground washed white. -->
    <headlight ambient="0.28 0.29 0.31" diffuse="0.32 0.32 0.32" specular="0.08 0.08 0.08"/>
    <quality shadowsize="4096" offsamples="8"/>
    <map shadowclip="2" shadowscale="1.5"/>
  </visual>

  <default>
    <!-- Rubber-on-asphalt: high sliding friction so the tire rolls rather
         than skids when the disturbance hits. -->
    <geom friction="1.0 0.005 0.0001"/>
    <default class="visual">
      <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"/>
    </default>
    <default class="collision">
      <geom group="3"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <!-- Brand palette (see overboard-web index.html :root tokens):
         ink #16232E, tire #23384A, amber #F2A24A, mint #2AAE97, cloud #F4F8F7 -->
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.82 0.87 0.90" rgb2="0.35 0.44 0.52"
             width="512" height="512"/>
    <!-- The two tones need real separation AND headroom. An earlier pass used
         the brand paper colours (0.07 apart in luminance) and the checker was
         invisible; raising them to ~0.95 then clipped to flat white under the
         ~1.33x total illumination, so the pattern only survived inside
         shadows. These albedos land just under saturation when lit. Neutral
         rather than blue, so the ground does not compete with the bumpers. -->
    <texture name="grid" type="2d" builtin="checker" rgb1="0.720 0.730 0.720"
             rgb2="0.560 0.580 0.570" width="512" height="512"/>
    <!-- Ground texture is a rendering necessity, not decoration: the scenario
         cameras track the board, so without ground detail at a human scale the
         board appears to hang motionless while actually travelling 4 m. The
         checker supplies the parallax that makes the motion legible.
         texrepeat is tuned by eye — MuJoCo's plane texture mapping is not
         simply repeats-over-the-plane, so 160 is what lands ~1 m squares
         against a 0.94 m board, not the 80 the arithmetic suggested. -->
    <material name="ground_mat" texture="grid" texrepeat="160 160" reflectance="0.05" specular="0.1"/>

    <material name="shell_mat" rgba="0.086 0.137 0.180 1" specular="0.35" shininess="0.45"/>
    <material name="bumper_mat" rgba="0.949 0.635 0.290 1" specular="0.25" shininess="0.3"/>
    <material name="footpad_mat" rgba="0.059 0.098 0.133 1" specular="0.05" shininess="0.05"/>
    <material name="platform_mat" rgba="0.165 0.682 0.592 1" specular="0.3" shininess="0.3"/>
    <material name="tire_mat" rgba="0.078 0.106 0.137 1" specular="0.12" shininess="0.15"/>

    <mesh name="front_enclosure" file="front_enclosure.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="rear_enclosure" file="rear_enclosure.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="front_bumper" file="front_bumper.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="rear_bumper" file="rear_bumper.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="front_footpad" file="front_footpad.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="rear_footpad" file="rear_footpad.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
    <mesh name="electronics_platform" file="electronics_platform.stl" scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light name="key" directional="true" pos="-1.5 -2.0 4.0" dir="0.35 0.45 -1"
           diffuse="0.75 0.74 0.72" specular="0.25 0.25 0.25" castshadow="true"/>
    <light name="fill" directional="true" pos="2.5 2.5 3.0" dir="-0.5 -0.5 -1"
           diffuse="0.30 0.32 0.36" specular="0.05 0.05 0.05" castshadow="false"/>

    <geom name="ground" type="plane" size="20 20 0.1" material="ground_mat"/>

    <!--
      Frame: everything that is not the hub motor — rails, enclosures, battery,
      controller, footpads, bumpers. Free joint, spawned with the axle exactly
      at tire radius so the model starts in contact and at rest.

      Mass 8.0 kg with the centre of mass 30 mm BELOW the axle. That sign is
      the whole point: the battery and controller live low in the rails, which
      is why a real driverless onewheel hangs stably upright instead of
      falling over. It is a weak restoring moment (m*g*l = 2.35 N*m/rad), so
      the board is only marginally stable — a modest push is enough to put the
      nose down, which is exactly the regime the impulse test probes.

      Pitch inertia is a box approximation over the mass-bearing span (the
      enclosures, +/-0.35 m) rather than the full nose-to-tail 0.938 m — the
      bumper tips are hollow ABS and carry almost nothing.
    -->
    <body name="frame" pos="0 0 0.1454">
      <freejoint name="frame_free"/>
      <inertial pos="0 0 -0.03" mass="8.0" diaginertia="0.040 0.400 0.420"/>

      <!-- IMU mounting point: on the electronics platform, just aft of the
           axle, axes aligned with the frame. Right-handed only (MuJoCo
           cannot represent a left-handed frame): forward = -x, up = +z,
           so right is derived, not asserted:
             (-x_hat) x y_hat = -z_hat  =>  right = +y. -->
      <site name="imu" pos="-0.06 0 -0.007" size="0.008" group="4"/>

      <!-- Openwheel shells: visual only. -->
      <geom name="front_enclosure_geom" class="visual" type="mesh" mesh="front_enclosure" material="shell_mat"/>
      <geom name="rear_enclosure_geom" class="visual" type="mesh" mesh="rear_enclosure" material="shell_mat"/>
      <geom name="front_footpad_geom" class="visual" type="mesh" mesh="front_footpad" euler="0 0 180" material="footpad_mat"/>
      <geom name="rear_footpad_geom" class="visual" type="mesh" mesh="rear_footpad" material="footpad_mat"/>
      <geom name="electronics_platform_geom" class="visual" type="mesh" mesh="electronics_platform" material="platform_mat"/>

      <!-- Bumpers: visual AND collision. These are the geoms that define the
           nose-strike angle, so they use their real convex hulls — no
           stand-in box, no tuned fudge factor. `toppled` is detected as a
           contact between either of these and the ground plane. -->
      <geom name="front_bumper_geom" type="mesh" mesh="front_bumper" material="bumper_mat"
            group="2" condim="3" friction="0.6 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="rear_bumper_geom" type="mesh" mesh="rear_bumper" material="bumper_mat"
            group="2" condim="3" friction="0.6 0.005 0.0001"/>

      <!--
        Hub wheel: 11.5 in OD x 6 in wide onewheel tire on a direct-drive hub
        motor. 4.5 kg is mostly stator iron, magnets and tire rubber out at the
        rim, so it is modelled as a hoop rather than a solid disc: radius of
        gyration 0.115 m (0.79 of the 0.1454 m outer radius) over a 0.15 m
        width, giving Iyy = m*a^2 and Ixx = Izz = m*a^2/2 + m*w^2/12.
        Its hinge IS the motor axis.
      -->
      <body name="wheel" pos="0 0 0">
        <!--
          Hinge axis is -Y, not +Y, and that sign is load-bearing. It makes
          positive joint velocity = rolling FORWARD and positive motor torque =
          forward acceleration, which is what BoardIo ICD 7.3 mandates:

              amps > 0  =>  forward wheel acceleration  =>  nose pitches up.

          With a +Y axis the model did the exact opposite (+current drove the
          board backwards and pitched the nose DOWN), which would have inverted
          the balance law the moment the Rust controller was wired in -- the
          single failure mode the ICD calls out as the most dangerous across
          this seam. Asserted by test_motor_sign_matches_icd; do not flip
          without changing the ICD.
        -->
        <!--
          WHEEL DRAG -- two terms, replacing an undocumented lumped
          `damping="0.08"`. That single viscous coefficient was typed in
          rather than derived, and real onewheel-class drag has THREE
          physically distinct terms with three different speed-scalings
          (constant, linear, quadratic); one coefficient cannot represent
          more than one of them. Measured against a hoverboard-class-vehicle
          "real total" drag table, the old lumped term was 3-5x too LOW at
          low speed (missing the constant term entirely) and had no
          provenance comment at all -- ADR-0011 named it "the only
          load-bearing constant in the MJCF with no provenance comment".

          Two of the three real terms belong on THIS joint -- rolling
          resistance and bearing/motor drag both act as a resisting TORQUE
          at the wheel. The third (rider aerodynamic drag, quadratic in
          speed) acts on the frame/rider BODY directly, not through the
          drivetrain, and does not belong on a hinge at all -- see
          `overboard_rider.xml`'s wheel_hinge comment for where it lives on
          that file, and this file's own header note (just above the
          `wheel` body) for why it is DELIBERATELY ABSENT here.

          ROLLING RESISTANCE -- `frictionloss`, CONSTANT (Coulomb).
          Crr = 0.02, the centre of a 0.015-0.025 working band for an 18 psi
          wide pneumatic tyre. Engineering Toolbox's generic pneumatic-tyre
          table runs 0.007-0.02 and trends HIGHER as pressure drops; this
          tyre is 18 psi and wide (the low-pressure, high-Crr end of that
          table), so the band used here is shifted up from the generic one
          rather than centred on it.
          Normal load is THIS model's own total mass at rest -- frame 8.0 kg
          + wheel 4.5 kg = 12.5 kg -> 122.6 N at this option's own gravity
          (9.81 m/s^2). Driverless: no rider mass on this load. (Compare
          overboard_rider.xml, where the same Crr multiplies the ridden
          load instead.)
              frictionloss = Crr * W * r
                           = 0.02 * 122.6 N * 0.1454 m = 0.3565 N*m

          BEARING + HUB-MOTOR IRON/WINDAGE LOSSES -- `damping`, LINEAR
          (viscous). 20-40 W of such losses at top speed (8.34 m/s, i.e.
          omega = v/r = 57.36 rad/s) implies a viscous coefficient of
          P/omega^2 = 0.006-0.012 N*m*s/rad -- an ORDER OF MAGNITUDE below
          the 0.08 this replaces, which was never derived from anything.
          Centre of that band: 0.009 N*m*s/rad. This term is a property of
          the drivetrain (bearings + motor), not of vehicle load, so it is
          the SAME value in overboard_rider.xml.

          WHAT WOULD REPLACE THIS AT THE HARDWARE GATE: a bench spin-down
          measurement of the actual wheel assembly -- the same method
          `sim/scenarios/bench_spinup.py` already fits `dof_damping`/
          `dof_frictionloss` FROM (on the desk-scale identification rig,
          not this board). This pair is a published-coefficient estimate,
          not a measurement of the real part, and should be replaced by one
          the moment a real wheel can be spun down on a bench.
        -->
        <joint name="wheel_hinge" type="hinge" axis="0 -1 0" pos="0 0 0"
               frictionloss="0.3565" damping="0.009"/>
        <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="4.5" diaginertia="0.0382 0.0595 0.0382"/>
        <geom name="wheel_geom" type="cylinder" size="0.1454 0.075" euler="90 0 0"
              material="tire_mat" condim="4"/>
      </body>

      <!-- Cameras ride the frame's subtree CoM (translate with the board, do
           not rotate with it) so the board stays framed as it rolls away. -->
      <camera name="side" mode="trackcom" pos="0 2.6 0.30" xyaxes="-1 0 0 0 0 1" fovy="24"/>
      <camera name="beauty" mode="trackcom" pos="-1.7 2.0 0.85"
              xyaxes="-2.0 -1.7 0 1.445 -1.700 6.89" fovy="26"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- The single motor, matching BoardIo ICD 7.3:
         positive current -> forward wheel acceleration -> nose pitches UP.
       Pitch is nose-up-positive throughout (ICD 10.1), so the stabilising
       balance term is current ~= -K*pitch with K > 0: a nose-DOWN excursion is
       negative pitch, and correcting it means driving the contact patch
       forward, i.e. positive current.

       CTRL IS TORQUE IN N*m, NOT AMPS. `gear="1"` means MuJoCo takes ctrl as
       the joint torque directly. The controller speaks amps, so the scenario
       multiplies by KT_NM_PER_A before writing here. ctrlrange is therefore
       derived, not chosen: 40 A limit x 0.7 N*m/A = 28 N*m.

       An earlier revision of this comment claimed pitch was nose-down-positive
       while also writing the law as -K*pitch, which in that convention is
       positive feedback. It was a comment, so nothing depended on it -- but a
       comment that contradicts itself about a sign is worse than none, because
       it eventually gets believed. -->
  <actuator>
    <motor name="wheel_motor" joint="wheel_hinge" gear="1" ctrlrange="-28 28" ctrllimited="true"/>
  </actuator>

  <!-- Sensor set mirrors the real board's IMU + drive telemetry, so the
       scenario reads the same signals the controller will. -->
  <sensor>
    <framequat name="frame_quat" objtype="body" objname="frame"/>
    <gyro name="frame_gyro" site="imu"/>
    <accelerometer name="frame_accel" site="imu"/>
    <jointpos name="wheel_pos" joint="wheel_hinge"/>
    <jointvel name="wheel_vel" joint="wheel_hinge"/>
    <actuatorfrc name="motor_torque" actuator="wheel_motor"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
